// S~S  SimpleSal signal starts  S~S

// [Time And Agency FSMs.note]          search in ssTEA source files for a reference to this file.
//
    Time really does function as an FSM.  The FSM generates a signal: a moment of Time happened.
    Every thing that exists, alive or not, perceives a signal from the Time FSM. Tick. Tick. Tick.

    A signal from Time is instantiated by perceiving very small time periods and counting them.

    ssTEA, which co-exists with the App, perceives the signal from Time.  This is the Time FSM.

    The App software is a signal algorithm interacting with FSMs on a periodic basis.  The period
    of the interactions is a function of the FSMs, which are physical devices or software APIs.

    The Time FSM signal is emitted to the Agency FSM based on the runtime configuration of ssTEA.
    When the Agency FSM perceives a Time FSM signal, a database is searched for Ready Events.

    The "Events are ready" condition is true when:

      1) the Time at which they are to occur has arrived;

      2) software has signaled that the Event should resume (the MAC is activated to process data);

      3) the interrupt controller has filtered through the interrupt tree and found a hot one.
//

// S~S  SimpleSal signal ends  S~S
